1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,340
Hello everyone, this is Ria!

2
00:00:04,340 --> 00:00:10,880
It's been so hot these days, how are you doing?

3
00:00:10,880 --> 00:00:14,140
How's the weather right now?

4
00:00:14,140 --> 00:00:17,920
It's summer, so it's really hot, right?

5
00:00:17,920 --> 00:00:26,820
A little while ago, my mom sent me a text message.

6
00:00:26,820 --> 00:00:30,120
Here's the weather, haha.

7
00:00:30,120 --> 00:00:32,490
Is it okay over there?

8
00:00:32,490 --> 00:00:36,530
I'm traveling right now.

9
00:00:36,530 --> 00:00:39,010
Have you caught a cold?

10
00:00:39,010 --> 00:00:47,840
I caught a pretty bad cold, so my mom is worried.

11
00:00:47,840 --> 00:01:00,370
Anyway, the photo my mom sent was so funny that I wanted to show it to you all.

12
00:01:00,370 --> 00:01:04,580
Here is some corn.

13
00:01:04,580 --> 00:01:06,270
Corn.

14
00:01:06,270 --> 00:01:09,950
The corn asked,

15
00:01:09,950 --> 00:01:12,730
"Where have you been?"

16
00:01:12,730 --> 00:01:18,090
The corn outside the door answers.

17
00:01:18,090 --> 00:01:19,620
Daegu.

18
00:01:19,620 --> 00:01:23,360
The corn turned into popcorn.

19
00:01:23,360 --> 00:01:30,070
Because the weather outside was so hot, it turned into popcorn.

20
00:01:30,070 --> 00:01:32,960
Isn't it so cute?

21
00:01:32,960 --> 00:01:42,140
This corn said it went to Daegu, but what is Daegu?

22
00:01:42,140 --> 00:01:48,150
Daegu is the name of a region in Korea.

23
00:01:48,150 --> 00:01:50,240
Region name.

24
00:01:50,240 --> 00:01:51,870
Region.

25
00:01:51,870 --> 00:02:01,490
From Seoul, it takes about 3 to 4 hours by car.

26
00:02:01,490 --> 00:02:03,740
KTX.

27
00:02:03,740 --> 00:02:12,820
There is a super fast train in Korea called the KTX.

28
00:02:12,820 --> 00:02:24,530
If you take it, it takes about 1 hour and 40 minutes from Seoul to Daegu.

29
00:02:24,530 --> 00:02:30,320
Daegu is the hottest city in Korea.

30
00:02:30,320 --> 00:02:32,030
Hot city.

31
00:02:32,030 --> 00:02:44,920
So in this photo, after the corn went to Daegu, it got so hot that it turned into popcorn.

32
00:02:44,920 --> 00:02:52,890
Whenever I think of Daegu, hot weather immediately comes to mind.

33
00:02:52,890 --> 00:02:55,780
Hot weather comes to mind.

34
00:02:55,780 --> 00:03:00,850
Do you know Daegu's nickname? Everyone?

35
00:03:00,850 --> 00:03:05,500
It's Daefrica. Daefrica!

36
00:03:05,500 --> 00:03:12,490
They combine Daegu and Africa to call it Daefrica.

37
00:03:12,490 --> 00:03:14,940
Isn't that so funny?

38
00:03:14,940 --> 00:03:22,140
If you look at the map of Korea, Daegu is here.

39
00:03:22,140 --> 00:03:28,250
But there is no sea around it at all.

40
00:03:28,250 --> 00:03:33,140
There is no sea around it at all.

41
00:03:33,140 --> 00:03:38,470
It is located far from the sea.

42
00:03:38,470 --> 00:03:43,390
This is called inland.

43
00:03:43,390 --> 00:03:46,140
Inland.

44
00:03:46,140 --> 00:03:55,620
Places like this tend to be hot in summer and cold in winter.

45
00:03:55,620 --> 00:04:02,140
I have been to Daegu only once since I was born.

46
00:04:02,140 --> 00:04:06,720
But that was in winter.

47
00:04:06,720 --> 00:04:10,430
I have never been there in summer.

48
00:04:10,430 --> 00:04:21,060
If I meet someone from Daegu, I think I would talk about the weather as small talk.

49
00:04:21,060 --> 00:04:26,310
"Oh, Daefrica! How is it living in Daegu?"

50
00:04:26,310 --> 00:04:29,370
"Is it really that hot?"

51
00:04:29,370 --> 00:04:32,580
I think I would ask like this.

52
00:04:32,580 --> 00:04:42,840
If you meet someone living in Daegu later, be sure to talk about the weather!

53
00:04:42,840 --> 00:04:48,660
It's already the end of August, everyone.

54
00:04:48,660 --> 00:04:50,140
End of August.

55
00:04:50,140 --> 00:04:52,520
The last part of August.

56
00:04:52,520 --> 00:04:56,610
This is called the end of August.

57
00:04:56,610 --> 00:04:58,160
End of August.

58
00:04:58,160 --> 00:05:04,130
It definitely feels less hot than the end of July.

59
00:05:04,130 --> 00:05:06,350
Feels less hot.

60
00:05:06,350 --> 00:05:12,140
Not hotter, but less hot.

61
00:05:12,140 --> 00:05:15,930
The end of July is midsummer.

62
00:05:15,930 --> 00:05:17,280
Midsummer.

63
00:05:17,280 --> 00:05:21,760
We learned these summer expressions last time, right?

64
00:05:21,760 --> 00:05:32,720
Back then, we talked a lot about European summer, but today we will talk more properly about summer.

65
00:05:32,720 --> 00:05:39,490
I wanted to make podcasts for each season.

66
00:05:39,490 --> 00:05:45,410
Last time, I made a podcast about spring.

67
00:05:45,410 --> 00:05:51,970
We talked a lot about flowers and cherry blossoms then.

68
00:05:51,970 --> 00:05:56,380
That was fun too, so please check it out.

69
00:05:56,380 --> 00:06:01,510
This time, it's my summer podcast!

70
00:06:01,510 --> 00:06:06,920
Then later, I will probably do an autumn podcast too, right?

71
00:06:06,920 --> 00:06:09,630
Please look forward to it!

72
00:06:09,630 --> 00:06:16,100
I am currently in a city in Japan called Osaka.

73
00:06:16,100 --> 00:06:20,000
The weather here is really hot too.

74
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:26,720
It was hot last month and it's still hot now.

75
00:06:26,720 --> 00:06:34,690
Honestly, I think it's hotter than summer in Korea.

76
00:06:34,690 --> 00:06:47,130
So today, I want to teach you some Korean expressions you can use when it's hot.

77
00:06:47,130 --> 00:06:50,200
Hot, it's hot.

78
00:06:50,200 --> 00:06:53,630
So hot, really hot.

79
00:06:53,630 --> 00:06:57,960
These are good, but something else!

80
00:06:57,960 --> 00:07:02,430
What else could there be?

81
00:07:02,430 --> 00:07:09,790
Koreans often use the phrase "죽겠다" (I'm dying) a lot.

82
00:07:09,790 --> 00:07:14,060
"I'm dying from the heat," "I'm dying from the heat."

83
00:07:14,060 --> 00:07:19,510
Usually, this "죽겠다" phrase is used a lot.

84
00:07:19,510 --> 00:07:21,880
"I'm tired to death."

85
00:07:21,880 --> 00:07:24,640
"I'm hungry to death."

86
00:07:24,640 --> 00:07:27,380
"I miss you to death."

87
00:07:27,380 --> 00:07:29,970
"I'm so cute to death."

88
00:07:29,970 --> 00:07:38,960
They use "죽겠다" after good words like this to emphasize a lot.

89
00:07:38,960 --> 00:07:44,910
I think it's similar in your language too.

90
00:07:44,910 --> 00:07:48,080
Using the phrase "죽겠다"!

91
00:07:48,080 --> 00:07:55,520
Now, here is a more native-like expression!

92
00:07:55,520 --> 00:08:01,750
There is a phrase "쪄죽겠다," "쪄죽겠어요."

93
00:08:01,750 --> 00:08:03,490
"쪄죽겠다"

94
00:08:03,490 --> 00:08:09,650
This is a word made by combining two words.

95
00:08:09,650 --> 00:08:12,480
"찌다" and "죽겠다"

96
00:08:12,480 --> 00:08:13,880
"쪄죽겠다"

97
00:08:13,880 --> 00:08:23,040
Originally, "찌다," "쪄요" is a word often used when cooking.

98
00:08:23,040 --> 00:08:25,610
I steam dumplings.

99
00:08:25,610 --> 00:08:28,130
I steam sweet potatoes.

100
00:08:28,130 --> 00:08:36,790
But when you steam dumplings and sweet potatoes like this, how do you cook them?

101
00:08:36,790 --> 00:08:43,560
You put it in a pot and cook it with hot air.

102
00:08:43,560 --> 00:08:57,420
It's so hot that it feels like we're inside this pot, so we use the expression 'jjida' (to steam).

103
00:08:57,420 --> 00:09:00,450
I'm going to steam to death. I'm really going to steam to death.

104
00:09:00,450 --> 00:09:04,240
I really feel like I'm going to steam to death.

105
00:09:04,240 --> 00:09:10,740
By the way, this kind of cooking is called 'jjim' (steamed dishes).

106
00:09:10,740 --> 00:09:12,600
Jjim dishes

107
00:09:12,600 --> 00:09:16,880
Dishes made by steaming, jjim dishes.

108
00:09:16,880 --> 00:09:19,250
Suddenly talking about cooking?

109
00:09:19,250 --> 00:09:26,110
My favorite jjim dish is steamed eggs.

110
00:09:26,110 --> 00:09:30,840
I really love steamed eggs...!

111
00:09:30,840 --> 00:09:37,060
I will share my steamed egg recipe later.

112
00:09:37,060 --> 00:09:40,500
I have my own recipe.

113
00:09:40,500 --> 00:09:46,460
Besides this, if you steam meat, it's meat jjim!

114
00:09:46,460 --> 00:09:52,000
If you steam fish, it's fish jjim.

115
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:55,260
There is also this expression.

116
00:09:55,260 --> 00:10:00,510
Jjim-tong heat (steaming pot heat)

117
00:10:00,510 --> 00:10:05,560
Now the word 'jjim' is a bit familiar, right?

118
00:10:05,560 --> 00:10:15,140
'Jjim-tong' refers to the container used to steam food when cooking.

119
00:10:15,140 --> 00:10:24,640
Because it feels as hot as being inside that container, it's called 'jjim-tong heat.'

120
00:10:24,640 --> 00:10:31,360
This expression is often used in news articles.

121
00:10:31,360 --> 00:10:42,270
Here it says, "End of jjim-tong heat, perfect weather to wear hanbok."

122
00:10:42,270 --> 00:10:46,510
'Je-gyeok' means just right or perfect.

123
00:10:46,510 --> 00:10:51,690
Perfect weather to wear hanbok, just right!

124
00:10:51,690 --> 00:10:57,710
The jjim-tong heat is over, so it's perfect to wear hanbok.

125
00:10:57,710 --> 00:11:04,130
What kind of food do you eat when it's hot in your country?

126
00:11:04,130 --> 00:11:07,970
Ice cream? Shaved ice? Cold noodles?

127
00:11:07,970 --> 00:11:12,430
There are various cool foods.

128
00:11:12,430 --> 00:11:23,010
Actually, in Korea, the most representative food that comes to mind in summer is chicken.

129
00:11:23,010 --> 00:11:24,460
Chicken

130
00:11:24,460 --> 00:11:30,910
But I suddenly have a concern.

131
00:11:30,910 --> 00:11:36,360
It's very random, but I have a concern.

132
00:11:36,360 --> 00:11:49,850
Actually, when pronouncing the sentence "It's chicken," it should originally be pronounced as "dal-gi-eyo." It should be "dal-gi-eyo."

133
00:11:49,850 --> 00:11:55,880
According to pronunciation rules, it should be "dal-gi-eyo."

134
00:11:55,880 --> 00:12:00,930
You already know this rule.

135
00:12:00,930 --> 00:12:08,430
The final consonant 'giyok' connects to the next syllable, so it becomes "dal-gi-eyo."

136
00:12:08,430 --> 00:12:14,710
I already know this well in my head.

137
00:12:14,710 --> 00:12:23,280
But actually, no Korean says "dal-gi-eyo."

138
00:12:23,280 --> 00:12:28,100
They just pronounce it like "da-gi-eyo."

139
00:12:28,100 --> 00:12:29,310
Da-gi-eyo.

140
00:12:29,310 --> 00:12:38,610
Broadcasters and announcers have to pronounce it according to the rules on TV.

141
00:12:38,610 --> 00:12:45,880
But in daily life, people often don't follow these rules.

142
00:12:45,880 --> 00:12:49,350
Dal-gi-eyo. Da-gi-eyo.

143
00:12:49,350 --> 00:12:57,470
I actually had many moments like this while recording my podcast.

144
00:12:57,470 --> 00:13:11,270
I was worried about whether to teach the original pronunciation or the pronunciation that Koreans actually use a lot.

145
00:13:11,270 --> 00:13:18,390
So I try to teach both pronunciations as much as possible.

146
00:13:18,390 --> 00:13:26,930
I will tell you in the video or leave it in the comments.

147
00:13:26,930 --> 00:13:31,260
Anyway, chicken, chicken, chicken!

148
00:13:31,260 --> 00:13:36,570
I even shared my concern counseling because of chicken.

149
00:13:36,570 --> 00:13:43,130
In Korea, people eat a lot of chicken in summer. Interesting, right?

150
00:13:43,130 --> 00:13:46,530
Why chicken all of a sudden?

151
00:13:46,530 --> 00:13:53,500
There is a summer culture you must know.

152
00:13:53,500 --> 00:13:58,530
It's called Sambok. Sambok!

153
00:13:58,530 --> 00:14:06,980
Sambok refers to the three hottest days in summer in Korea.

154
00:14:06,980 --> 00:14:11,670
They are between July and August.

155
00:14:11,670 --> 00:14:16,740
These dates change every year.

156
00:14:16,740 --> 00:14:18,940
They change every year.

157
00:14:18,940 --> 00:14:21,880
I don't know when they are either.

158
00:14:21,880 --> 00:14:29,060
But if you go to Naver, sometimes the Sambok dates pop up.

159
00:14:29,060 --> 00:14:35,740
There are cute characters, so you can know right away.

160
00:14:35,740 --> 00:14:40,340
Sambok has three days.

161
00:14:40,340 --> 00:14:44,150
The first day is Chobok.

162
00:14:44,150 --> 00:14:45,620
Chobok

163
00:14:45,620 --> 00:14:48,930
It marks the start of summer.

164
00:14:48,930 --> 00:14:52,730
The second day is Jungbok.

165
00:14:52,730 --> 00:14:53,820
Jungbok

166
00:14:53,820 --> 00:14:55,350
Middle

167
00:14:55,350 --> 00:14:59,930
Like the 'jung' in middle school.

168
00:14:59,930 --> 00:15:02,970
It uses the same Chinese character.

169
00:15:02,970 --> 00:15:06,950
The third day is Malbok.

170
00:15:06,950 --> 00:15:08,120
Malbok.

171
00:15:08,120 --> 00:15:12,950
I said these are the three hottest days.

172
00:15:12,950 --> 00:15:23,360
So Koreans thought of this period as the hardest time in summer.

173
00:15:23,360 --> 00:15:25,430
The hardest time.

174
00:15:25,430 --> 00:15:34,950
So to protect their bodies, they eat healthy food.

175
00:15:34,950 --> 00:15:41,800
As I mentioned earlier, they eat chicken the most.

176
00:15:41,800 --> 00:15:49,660
Especially, the most eaten chicken dish is Samgyetang.

177
00:15:49,660 --> 00:15:51,490
Samgyetang

178
00:15:51,490 --> 00:15:56,280
The 'sam' in Samgyetang means ginseng.

179
00:15:56,280 --> 00:15:57,750
Ginseng

180
00:15:57,750 --> 00:16:01,650
Korean ginseng is famous.

181
00:16:01,650 --> 00:16:09,360
It's really healthy food, though it's bitter and not tasty.

182
00:16:09,360 --> 00:16:14,040
'Gye' means chicken.

183
00:16:14,040 --> 00:16:18,040
It's the Chinese character for chicken.

184
00:16:18,040 --> 00:16:22,930
'Tang' means soup or broth dish.

185
00:16:22,930 --> 00:16:38,320
Samgyetang is a dish where a young chicken is stuffed with glutinous rice, garlic, jujube, ginseng, and boiled thoroughly.

186
00:16:38,320 --> 00:16:40,700
Boiled thoroughly

187
00:16:40,700 --> 00:16:49,050
It's already very hot in summer, so why do they eat hot food?

188
00:16:49,050 --> 00:16:52,900
This is also Korean culture.

189
00:16:52,900 --> 00:16:57,840
It's called 'iyeolchiyeol.'

190
00:16:57,840 --> 00:17:00,120
Iyeolchiyeol

191
00:17:00,120 --> 00:17:05,140
It means to fight heat with heat.

192
00:17:05,140 --> 00:17:15,350
When it's hot, you eat even hotter food to overcome the heat.

193
00:17:15,350 --> 00:17:31,340
So maybe you've seen it before, but in Korean dramas, middle-aged men always eat hot food.

194
00:17:31,340 --> 00:17:35,370
Then they make sounds.

195
00:17:35,370 --> 00:17:37,830
Ah, that's refreshing! (lol)

196
00:17:37,830 --> 00:17:42,610
They say it's refreshing while eating hot food.

197
00:17:42,610 --> 00:17:46,500
This is iyeolchiyeol.

198
00:17:46,500 --> 00:18:05,980
Originally, the word 'siwonhada' (refreshing) in Korean is used when the temperature is cold, but when you say it while eating hot food, it means a kind of refreshing feeling.

199
00:18:05,980 --> 00:18:08,510
A feeling of refreshment!

200
00:18:08,510 --> 00:18:12,790
Especially Korean middle-aged men use it a lot.

201
00:18:12,790 --> 00:18:15,370
Ah, si~~~wonhada!

202
00:18:15,370 --> 00:18:19,480
They also use it a lot in hot jjimjilbangs (Korean saunas).

203
00:18:19,480 --> 00:18:21,400
Si~~~wonhada!

204
00:18:21,400 --> 00:18:26,740
They seem to stretch out the word 'siwon.'

205
00:18:26,740 --> 00:18:32,760
Actually, I don't really like Samgyetang that much.

206
00:18:32,760 --> 00:18:36,630
It feels like a food for older people?

207
00:18:36,630 --> 00:18:44,660
Of course, I'm an adult too, but it feels like a food more for older adults?

208
00:18:44,660 --> 00:18:50,420
It's a food that I think my parents would like.

209
00:18:50,420 --> 00:18:59,060
I wonder if someday I will properly enjoy Samgyetang.

210
00:18:59,060 --> 00:19:15,600
Before I traveled, the first hot day, Chobok, was July 15, 2026.

211
00:19:15,600 --> 00:19:19,690
At that time, I was in Korea.

212
00:19:19,690 --> 00:19:24,110
My mom contacted me then.

213
00:19:24,110 --> 00:19:29,360
"Ria, it's Chobok, so I bought chicken dish!"

214
00:19:29,360 --> 00:19:32,930
So I was really excited.

215
00:19:32,930 --> 00:19:35,910
Wow, is it fried chicken?

216
00:19:35,910 --> 00:19:38,620
I hoped it was fried chicken...!

217
00:19:38,620 --> 00:19:43,960
But when I opened it, it was Samgyetang.

218
00:19:43,960 --> 00:19:51,670
Actually, Samgyetang is a much more expensive dish.

219
00:19:51,670 --> 00:20:01,320
With ingredients like ginseng, healthy food is called 'boyangsik' in Korean.

220
00:20:01,320 --> 00:20:03,030
Boyangsik

221
00:20:03,030 --> 00:20:08,470
There are various types of boyangsik.

222
00:20:08,470 --> 00:20:13,290
Usually, these boyangsik dishes are a bit expensive.

223
00:20:13,290 --> 00:20:21,960
But since I don't like these boyangsik dishes yet, I was a bit disappointed.

224
00:20:21,960 --> 00:20:28,950
So I said, "Ah, what! It's not chicken! Ah, what..." (lol)

225
00:20:28,950 --> 00:20:33,050
Do I seem like a child to you all?

226
00:20:33,050 --> 00:20:36,410
Do I seem like an immature daughter?

227
00:20:36,410 --> 00:20:38,040
Immature daughter?

228
00:20:38,040 --> 00:20:48,120
Then my mom said, "This is healthier and more expensive. Just eat it!" she said.

229
00:20:48,120 --> 00:20:55,980
Anyway, talking about Samgyetang suddenly reminded me of something.

230
00:20:55,980 --> 00:21:03,960
Do you know the famous Korean cup ramen called 'Yukgaejang'?

231
00:21:03,960 --> 00:21:11,950
This is really almost the second most famous ramen after Shin Ramyun.

232
00:21:11,950 --> 00:21:17,380
It's really delicious, so I think you all will like it too.

233
00:21:17,380 --> 00:21:23,460
But this year, a new flavor came out.

234
00:21:23,460 --> 00:21:28,120
It's the samgyetang (ginseng chicken soup) flavor! Amazing!

235
00:21:28,120 --> 00:21:36,040
But as far as I know, this flavor was originally only sold in Japan.

236
00:21:36,040 --> 00:21:47,100
It's a Korean company and Korean cup ramen, but it wasn't sold in Korea, only in Japan.

237
00:21:47,100 --> 00:21:48,980
Samgyetang flavor.

238
00:21:48,980 --> 00:21:50,900
Isn't that interesting?

239
00:21:50,900 --> 00:21:57,820
Finally! It says it's finally released domestically.

240
00:21:57,820 --> 00:22:12,090
"Domestic release" means that it has started to be sold and serviced for the first time inside Korea.

241
00:22:12,090 --> 00:22:20,840
I think it would taste even better if you add something like chicken breast to it.

242
00:22:20,840 --> 00:22:27,350
Actually, many Koreans eat it that way.

243
00:22:27,350 --> 00:22:31,730
I want to try it soon too.

244
00:22:31,730 --> 00:22:41,610
Since Koreans like spicy food, I think that's why it wasn't sold in Korea until now.

245
00:22:41,610 --> 00:22:52,060
They like spicy food and red broth, but this cup ramen doesn't have red broth.

